# Local development secrets — copy to `.dev.vars` and fill in.
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# `.dev.vars` is ignored by Git; this example file is the only one tracked, so
# every value here is an obvious placeholder. Use your NON-PRODUCTION Discord
# application only. Production credentials never belong on a developer machine.
#
# Find all three on the Discord Developer Portal (https://discord.com/developers/applications):
#   DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY     — General Information > Public Key
#   DISCORD_APPLICATION_ID — General Information > Application ID
#   DISCORD_TOKEN          — Bot > Token (Reset Token to see it; it is shown once)
#
# `wrangler dev` loads only the names declared in `secrets.required` in
# wrangler.jsonc, and warns about any that are missing.
DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY="replace-me-with-your-non-prod-application-public-key"
DISCORD_APPLICATION_ID="replace-me-with-your-non-prod-application-id"
DISCORD_TOKEN="replace-me-with-your-non-prod-bot-token"

# Read by `npm run register:non-prod`, not by the Worker: the test guild that
# receives guild-scoped command registrations. Right-click the server in Discord
# with Developer Mode enabled > Copy Server ID. The registration script reads it
# from the shell environment, so export it (or `set -a; source .dev.vars`)
# before running the script.
DISCORD_GUILD_ID="replace-me-with-your-test-guild-id"
